---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';

const entry = {
  "id": "harvard_vi4_495",
  "series": "Band VI 4 (1677 - 1690)",
  "seq": 495,
  "text": "[[2163.3]] <b>389.  De controversiis sacris generalibus [1. Hälfte 1677 (?)]</b>\n [[2163.4]] <tab/>[1. Hälfte 1677 (?)]\n[[2163.7]] <tit2><b>De controversiis sacris generalibus</b></tit2> </p>\n<p> [[2163.8]] <b>Controversia</b> est hoc loco quaestio in aliquo judicio agitata. </p>\n<p> [[2163.9]] <b>Judicium</b> est status rationibus certantium sub exitus spe. </p>\n<p> [[2163.10]] Opponitur Judicium Bello. <Fn><i>Anstreichung von</i> <b>Judicium</b> <i>bis</i> Bello.</Fn> </p>\n<p> [[2163.11]] <b>Bellum</b> est <i>status per vim certantium.</i> </p>\n<p> [[2163.12]] Nihil necesse est in definitione Belli addi <b>spem exitus</b>, quemadmodum eam\n[[2163.13]] addidimus in definitione Judicii. Nam ipsa natura certaminis per vim talis est, ut plerumque\n[[2163.14]] sub finem ejus appareat quis sit victor. At cum rationibus certatur raro sub certaminis\n[[2163.15]] finem apparet penes quem sit veritas aut victoria. </p>\n<p> [[2163.16]] Judicium ergo a <b>Disputatione</b> in eo differt, quod in judicio sub certaminis\n[[2163.17]] initium spes est ad exitum perveniri posse; at cum Disputatio inchoatur sine judice sive\n[[2163.18]] norma verendum est ne nihil agatur, sed idem qui ante fuit, sit sub finem disputationis\n[[2163.19]] rerum animorumque status. </p>\n<p> [[2164.1]] Quare Disputationes ad <b>exercitium</b> utiles esse possunt, at cum <b>serio</b> exitus\n[[2164.2]] certaminis quaeritur, ad judicium aliquod confugere debemus. </p>\n<p> [[2164.3]] <b>Exitus certaminis</b> est, cum post certamen res in eum pervenit statum, ut certari\n[[2164.4]] amplius facile non possit. </p>\n<p> [[2164.5]] Hic enim est <b>scopus certaminis serius</b>, ut tandem certaminum fiat finis. </p>\n<p> [[2164.6]] <b>Exitus certaminis</b> generalior est quam <b>victoria</b>, nam esse potest certaminum\n[[2164.7]] finis, neutro vincente. Quando scilicet ambo cecidere, quando ambo superstites atque\n[[2164.8]] integri anceps certamen pace diremere; quando neuter alterum convicit, quando apparet\n[[2164.9]] inane fuisse certamen, controversiae scilicet statu non in rebus aut sententiis, sed verbis\n[[2164.10]] posito, quibus semel declaratis quaestio cessavit. </p>\n<p> [[2164.11]] <b>Certamen</b> est experimentum virium eo animo utrinque captum, ut appareat uter sit\n[[2164.12]] potentior. </p>\n<p> [[2164.13]] Cum autem qui animo atque intelligentia praediti sunt nihil frustra faciant, patet\n[[2164.14]] certantibus, propositum esse aliquod praemium certaminis sive <b>Brabeum</b>, sive hoc ipsa\n[[2164.15]] victoris gloria sit, sive aliud quoddam lucrum cujus spe ardor certantium animatur. </p>\n<p> [[2164.16]] Certari potest viribus ingenii aut corporis; imo et fortuna. Nam ille quoque qui\n[[2164.17]] fortunatior est censeri potest potentior. Potens enim est, qui amicum habet potentem,\n[[2164.18]] nempe fortunam. </p>\n<p> [[2164.19]] <b>Spes</b> exitus consistit in tali certaminis natura, ut judicari possit expectari exitum\n[[2164.20]] debere. Itaque non hic de illa spe loquor, quam sibi quisque fingere potest, sed quam res\n[[2164.21]] ipsa suppeditat, sive de sperandi causa, quam Galli vocant <i>apparence</i> perinde ac si\n[[2164.22]] dixissem judicium esse statum rationibus certantium sub justa exitus spe. </p>\n<p> [[2164.23]] Ut ergo controversias quae in judicio sive sacro sive civili nasci possunt penitus\n[[2164.24]] intelligamus, consideranda erit persona certantium, praemium certaminis, spes exitus ex\n[[2164.25]] ipsis certandi legibus, ac denique rationes quibus certatur. Nam tractatio de <b>judice sive\n[[2164.26]] norma</i>, sub tractatione de <b>spe exitus</b> continetur. </p>\n<p> [[2164.27]] Circa certantes quaestio esse potest, an <b>personam</b> habeant standi in judicio, sive\n[[2164.28]] an audiri debeant. Quae sane dubitatio etiam in sacris controversiis obtinet. Quaeritur\n[[2164.29]] enim an sit cujusvis etiam Laici in has res accuratius inquirere, scripturam sacram legere\n[[2165.1]] aliaque vetustatis documenta excutere; controversiam de religione publice movere, et\n[[2165.2]] reformationem flagitare: an non qui talia privatim suae instructionis causa suscipit,\n[[2165.3]] eruditus aut etiam peculiariter illuminatus esse debeat. Et an is qui publice actionem\n[[2165.4]] instituere de his rebus audet, peculiari missione divina sive ordinaria sive extraordinaria\n[[2165.5]] debeat esse instructus. </p>\n<p> [[2165.6]] Huc <Fn><i>Durch Anstreichung hervorgehobener Absatz</i></Fn> ergo pertinet quaestio de permittenda aut prohibenda scripturae sacrae lectione;\n[[2165.7]] de libris prohibitis et indice expurgatorio, de fide implicita simplicium; de jure puniendi\n[[2165.8]] dubitantes disputantes, negantes, haereticos, apostatas, relapsos; deque inquisitoribus\n[[2165.9]] haereticae pravitatis. De admittendis ad disputationes, colloquiaque haereticis, et salvo\n[[2165.10]] conductu ipsis concedendo ac fide servanda, denique de Missione ordinaria, extraordinariave\n[[2165.11]] ad docendum necessaria. Semper enim in his quaeritur de jure admittendi aliquos\n[[2165.12]] ad controversias de religione privatas publicasve aut ab iis repellendi. </p>\n<p> [[2165.13]] <b>Praemium Certaminis</b> per rationes suscepti est persuasio. Quae persuasio est\n[[2165.14]] vel adversarii ipsius vel aliorum, audientium. <b>Persuasio adversarii</b> maximum est\n[[2165.15]] certaminis per rationes praemium. Ea enim demum plena victoria est, quae ut ille ait,\n[[2165.16]] <i>Confessos animo quoque subjugat hostes</i>, et vero tunc neque judicis autoritate, neque\n[[2165.17]] militari executione neque poenae metu, opus est, ad rem nostram consequendam confessionemve\n[[2165.18]] adversario extorquendam. </p>\n<p> [[2165.19]] <b>Persuasio auditorum</b> eam vim habet, tum ut pudore ac metu quodam contineatur\n[[2165.20]] adversarius si quid ultra moliri velit, tum ut applaudentium judicio atque autoritate\n[[2165.21]] quod volumus consequamur. </p>\n<p> [[2165.22]] Caeterum Persuasione quaerimus vel bonum aliquod nostrum vel alienum: <b>Bonum\n[[2165.23]] nostrum</i>, gloria, consecutio praemii aut honoris certantibus propositi; vel etiam rei\n[[2165.24]] nostrae de qua alius controversiam fecerat; securitas nostra per poenam improborum\n[[2165.25]] stabilita. </p>\n<p> [[2166.1]] <b>Bonum alienum</b> persuasione quaerimus, cum audientes meliores reddere conamur;\n[[2166.2]] quamquam fructus inde etiam in nos redundet, est enim inter caritatis opera,\n[[2166.3]] errantes docere. Caritati autem maxima praemia a Deo constituta esse certum est. </p>\n<p> [[2166.4]] Spes exitus pendet ex certitudine habendi notam victoriae sensibilem. </p>\n<p> [[2166.5]] Nota sensibilis, quae nulli disputationi subjecta est, exempli causa color aliquis\n[[2166.6]] oblatus ater an albus sit, non disputant homines, et omnino de iis quae praesenti sensu\n[[2166.7]] percipiuntur ab omnibus certamen nullum est, quoniam obvius quisque vocatus statim\n[[2166.8]] pronuntiaret pro victore vero. Exempli causa si tua sagitta propior scopo fixa haereat\n[[2166.9]] quam mea, nulla quaestio est de victoria, nam obvius quisque vocatus, statim dicet\n[[2166.10]] sagittam tuam esse propiorem etsi ignoret tuam esse. </p>\n<p> [[2166.11]] Modus perveniendi ad notam sensibilem in judiciis pendet ex judicii modo. Quaeritur\n[[2166.12]] autem nota sensibilis vel veritatis, vel persuasionis. Si persuasionis, quae alia haberi\n[[2166.13]] potest, quam approbatio auditorum, quae utique res est sensibilis, idque sufficit in\n[[2166.14]] civilibus, ubi auditorum autoritate atque assensu consequimur quod certando quaesivimus,\n[[2166.15]] gloriam scilicet aut rem. At in sacris ubi nota <b>sensibilis veritatis</b> quaeritur non\n[[2166.16]] sufficit persuasio, neque approbatio auditorum, nisi illi sint infallibiles in argumento de\n[[2166.17]] quo agitur. </p>\n<p> [[2166.18]] Notae veritatis sunt ratio aut autoritas. Autoritas divina aut humana. Item muta aut\n[[2166.19]] vocalis. Autoritas divina muta, est revelationis praeteritae relatio. Autoritas divina vocalis,\n[[2166.20]] est revelatio praesens, per miraculum aliquod testata. Huc pertinet et probatio per\n[[2166.21]] ignem, aquam, sortem, felicitatem temporalem, aliasque notas, quarum fundamentum est\n[[2166.22]] providentiae consentaneum esse ut Deus aliquod signum det; sive subsistens, sive novum\n[[2166.23]] cum nobis id postulare libuerit, quorum posterius minus probabile. Autoritas humana est\n[[2166.24]] aut de iis circa quae testimonium alicujus recipitur, aut de iis circa quae nemini credi\n[[2166.25]] necesse est nisi habeat privilegium infallibilitatis. Utraque est rursus muta vocalisve.\n[[2166.26]] Patrum exempli causa muta est autoritas, potest enim disputari de explicatione. Autoritas\n[[2166.27]] humana est vel singularis, vel universitatis, ut integri alicujus seculi, familiae, sectae,\n[[2166.28]] gentis. Autoritas hodierna sola vocalis est, estque aut hominis cujusdam aut gentis, aut\n[[2166.29]] etiam multitudinis infallibilis. Infallibilitas pendere potest aut a divina gratia, aut a rei\n[[2166.30]] natura, ut cum aliquis de re facti testatur nec est cur credamus corruptum ejus judicium,\n[[2166.31]] aut etiam scimus corrumpi non potuisse. Exempli causa testimonium alicujus multitu\n[[2167.1]] dinis de rebus facti. Talis est consensus perpetuus Christianorum ab omnibus seculis, talis\n[[2167.2]] est etiam sententia aliqua hodierna recepta, circa rem aliquam facti, quoties non potest\n[[2167.3]] ostendi mutatio, sequitur enim eam factam non fuisse. Huc pertinet principium Arnaldi.\n[[2167.4]] Nota quaedam infallibilitatis gratia divina a Deo datae foret necessitas ordinatoris ad\n[[2167.5]] sacramentorum ordinationem, ita enim illi qui successionem haberent, haberent praesumtionem.\n[[2167.6]] Nota omnes hae autoritates, excepta revelationis hodiernae vel hominis aut\n[[2167.7]] concilii infallibilis sunt mutae, seu explicatione alterius nonnunquam ratiocinationeve aut\n[[2167.8]] alio judice indigentes. Nota huc referenda Masenii et Veronii <i>methodus</i>, qui volebant\n[[2167.9]] reducere protestantes ad probandam confessionem ex scriptura sine ratiocinationibus ad\n[[2167.10]] quaestionem scilicet de autoritate et scriptura vocali. </p>\n<p> [[2167.11]] Rationes sunt aut probationes, aut praesumtiones, aut semiprobationes seu probabilitates.\n[[2167.12]] Quae ut faciant notam sensibilem certa forma proponendae sunt; ut appareat\n[[2167.13]] evidentia veritatis. Et hac forma inprimis opus est, cum de ipso judice vel autoritate muta\n[[2167.14]] vel vocali stabilienda agitur. Ultimus enim de judice judex ratio est. </p>\n",
  "number": "389.",
  "title": "De controversiis sacris generalibus",
  "date": "[1. Hälfte 1677 (?)]"
};
---

<Layout title={`${entry.title || entry.id} - Leibniz Writings`}>
  <article data-pagefind-body>
    <header class="entry-header">
      <h1 data-pagefind-meta="title">{entry.title || entry.id}</h1>

      <div class="entry-meta">
        {entry.date && (
          <div class="meta-item">
            <strong>Date:</strong>
            <span data-pagefind-meta="date">{entry.date}</span>
          </div>
        )}

        <div class="meta-item">
          <strong>Series:</strong>
          <span data-pagefind-meta="series">{entry.series}</span>
        </div>

        {entry.number && (
          <div class="meta-item">
            <strong>Number:</strong>
            <span data-pagefind-meta="number">{entry.number}</span>
          </div>
        )}

        <div class="meta-item">
          <strong>Sequence:</strong>
          <span>{entry.seq}</span>
        </div>
      </div>
    </header>

    <!-- View Toggle -->
    <div class="view-controls">
      <button id="translation-view-btn" class="view-btn active">Translation View</button>
      <button id="scholarly-view-btn" class="view-btn">Scholarly View</button>
    </div>

    <div class="entry-content" data-pagefind-meta="excerpt:389. De controversiis sacris generalibus [1. Hälfte 1677 (?)] [1. Hälfte 1677 (?)] De controversiis sacris generalibus Controversia est hoc loco quaestio in aliquo judicio agitata. Judicium est status rationibus certantium sub exitus spe. Opponitur Judicium Bello. Anstreichung von Judicium bis Bello...">

      <!-- Translation-friendly continuous text -->
      <div id="translation-view" class="text-view active">
        <div class="translation-notice">
          <p><strong>📖 Translation View:</strong> Continuous text optimized for browser translation tools.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="continuous-text" translate="yes" lang="unknown" id="continuous-text">
          <!-- Will be populated by JavaScript -->
        </div>
        <div class="line-numbers-reference" id="line-numbers-ref">
          <!-- Will be populated by JavaScript -->
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Detailed scholarly view with line numbers -->
      <div id="scholarly-view" class="text-view">
        <div class="scholarly-notice">
          <p><strong>📚 Scholarly View:</strong> Original text with line number annotations preserved.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="annotated-text" id="annotated-text">
          {entry.text.split('\n').map(paragraph =>
            paragraph.trim() && <p set:html={paragraph} />
          )}
        </div>
      </div>

    </div>
  </article>

  <nav class="entry-navigation">
    <a href="/" class="back-link">← Back to Search</a>
  </nav>
</Layout>

<style>
  .entry-header {
    margin-bottom: 30px;
    padding-bottom: 20px;
    border-bottom: 2px solid var(--border);
  }

  .entry-header h1 {
    color: var(--primary);
    font-size: 2rem;
    margin-bottom: 20px;
    line-height: 1.3;
  }

  .entry-meta {
    display: grid;
    gap: 8px;
    font-size: 0.95rem;
  }

  .meta-item {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-start;
    gap: 8px;
  }

  .meta-item strong {
    color: var(--text);
    min-width: 80px;
  }

  .meta-item span {
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    flex: 1;
  }

  .entry-content {
    margin-bottom: 40px;
  }

  .text-content {
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 30px;
    line-height: 1.7;
  }

  .text-content :global(p) {
    margin-bottom: 16px;
  }

  .text-content :global(p:last-child) {
    margin-bottom: 0;
  }

  .text-content :global(b) {
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--primary);
  }

  .entry-navigation {
    border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding-top: 20px;
  }

  .back-link {
    color: var(--primary);
    text-decoration: none;
    font-weight: 500;
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    gap: 8px;
    transition: color 0.2s;
  }

  .back-link:hover {
    color: var(--primary-dark);
    text-decoration: underline;
  }

  /* View Toggle Controls */
  .view-controls {
    display: flex;
    gap: 8px;
    margin-bottom: 20px;
    border-bottom: 1px solid var(--border);
    padding-bottom: 20px;
  }

  .view-btn {
    background: transparent;
    border: 2px solid var(--border);
    padding: 8px 16px;
    border-radius: 6px;
    cursor: pointer;
    font-size: 14px;
    font-weight: 500;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    transition: all 0.2s;
  }

  .view-btn:hover {
    border-color: var(--primary);
    color: var(--primary);
  }

  .view-btn.active {
    background: var(--primary);
    border-color: var(--primary);
    color: white;
  }

  /* Text Views */
  .text-view {
    display: none;
  }

  .text-view.active {
    display: block;
  }

  /* Translation View */
  .translation-notice, .scholarly-notice {
    background: var(--background);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 6px;
    padding: 12px;
    margin-bottom: 20px;
    font-size: 14px;
  }

  .continuous-text {
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 2px solid var(--primary);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 25px;
    line-height: 1.8;
    font-size: 16px;
    color: var(--text);
    margin-bottom: 15px;
  }

  .line-numbers-reference {
    color: var(--text-secondary);
    font-size: 13px;
    padding: 10px;
    background: var(--background);
    border-radius: 4px;
  }

  /* Scholarly View */
  .annotated-text {
    background: var(--surface);
    border: 1px solid var(--border);
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 20px;
  }

  .text-line {
    display: flex;
    align-items: flex-start;
    margin-bottom: 8px;
    gap: 12px;
  }

  .line-refs {
    display: flex;
    flex-wrap: wrap;
    gap: 4px;
    min-width: 80px;
    flex-shrink: 0;
  }

  .line-num {
    background: var(--primary);
    color: white;
    padding: 2px 6px;
    border-radius: 4px;
    font-size: 11px;
    font-weight: 600;
    font-family: monospace;
  }

  .line-content {
    flex: 1;
    line-height: 1.6;
  }

  .line-content :global(b) {
    font-weight: 600;
    color: var(--primary);
  }

  .line-content :global(i) {
    font-style: italic;
    color: var(--text-secondary);
  }

  @media (min-width: 768px) {
    .entry-meta {
      grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
    }
  }

  @media (max-width: 768px) {
    .view-controls {
      flex-direction: column;
    }

    .text-line {
      flex-direction: column;
      gap: 6px;
    }

    .line-refs {
      min-width: auto;
    }
  }
</style>

<script>
  // Process text for translation view
  function processTextForTranslation(text) {
    if (!text) return { translationText: '', lineNumbers: [] };

    const lines = text.split('\n');
    let translationText = '';
    const lineNumbers = [];

    lines.forEach(line => {
      const trimmedLine = line.trim();
      if (!trimmedLine) return;

      // Extract line numbers
      const lineNumberMatch = trimmedLine.match(/\[\[(\d+\.\d+)\]\]/g);
      const extractedNumbers = lineNumberMatch ?
        lineNumberMatch.map(m => m.replace(/\[|\]/g, '')) : [];

      // Remove line number markers and HTML tags for continuous text
      let contentOnly = trimmedLine
        .replace(/\[\[\d+\.\d+\]\]/g, '')
        .replace(/<\/?[^>]+(>|$)/g, '')
        .replace(/\s+/g, ' ')
        .trim();

      if (contentOnly) {
        if (translationText) translationText += ' ';
        translationText += contentOnly;
        lineNumbers.push(...extractedNumbers);
      }
    });

    return {
      translationText: translationText.trim(),
      lineNumbers: lineNumbers
    };
  }

  // View switching and text processing
  document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', () => {
    const translationBtn = document.getElementById('translation-view-btn');
    const scholarlyBtn = document.getElementById('scholarly-view-btn');
    const translationView = document.getElementById('translation-view');
    const scholarlyView = document.getElementById('scholarly-view');
    const continuousTextDiv = document.getElementById('continuous-text');
    const lineNumbersRef = document.getElementById('line-numbers-ref');

    // Process the text for translation view
    if (continuousTextDiv && lineNumbersRef && entry && entry.text) {
      const processed = processTextForTranslation(entry.text);
      continuousTextDiv.textContent = processed.translationText;

      if (processed.lineNumbers.length > 0) {
        lineNumbersRef.innerHTML = `<small><strong>Line numbers:</strong> ${processed.lineNumbers.join(', ')}</small>`;
      }
    }

    // View switching functionality
    if (translationBtn && scholarlyBtn && translationView && scholarlyView) {
      translationBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
        translationBtn.classList.add('active');
        scholarlyBtn.classList.remove('active');
        translationView.classList.add('active');
        scholarlyView.classList.remove('active');
      });

      scholarlyBtn.addEventListener('click', () => {
        scholarlyBtn.classList.add('active');
        translationBtn.classList.remove('active');
        scholarlyView.classList.add('active');
        translationView.classList.remove('active');
      });
    }
  });
</script>